      "id": 1,
      "text": "Patrick McGarry founded Walker in 1896 after the railroad reached the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walker%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Walker was named for lumber magnate Thomas Barlow Walker to attract his Red River Lumber Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Walker,_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Walker became the county seat of Cass County in 1897, just a year after its founding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/Walker,_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1907, Walker opened the Ah‑Gwah‑Ching Center, a tuberculosis facility that cared for patients with rest and nutrition.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walker%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The former tuberculosis complex was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2001.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walker%2C_Minnesota"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The Cass County Museum, operated by the Cass County Historical Society, sits in Walker and showcases regional history.",
